🖥️ Operating Systems Introduction — Hinglish Notes
1. Operating System (OS) kya hai?

OS = Computer ka core software jo hardware, applications aur users ke beech coordination karta hai.

Examples:

Windows
Linux
macOS
Android
iOS
🧠 Airport Analogy ✈️
Hardware = Airport ki physical infrastructure
Applications = Airlines/passengers
OS = Air Traffic Control

OS decide karta hai ki resources kaise use honge aur different programs kaise safely run karenge.

Without OS?

Har application ko directly:

CPU
RAM
Storage
Devices
Security

manage karna padta → conflicts aur instability bahut easily ho sakti hai.

🔐 2. System Privilege Layers

Modern OS mein permissions ke different levels hote hain.

Kernel Space

Highly privileged area.

Yahan kernel run karta hai.

Kernel ko direct access hota hai:

CPU
Memory
Storage
Hardware
🧠 Remember:

Kernel Space = Maximum privilege

User Space

Yahan normal applications run karti hain.

Examples:

Browser
Music player
Games
Text editor

Applications directly hardware access nahi karti.

Agar application ko file read karni hai ya sound play karna hai, toh wo system call ke through kernel se request karti hai.

🧠 Remember:

User Space → System Call → Kernel Space → Hardware

🛡️ 3. Privilege Separation kyun?

Security aur stability ke liye.

Agar har application ko direct hardware access mil jaaye, toh ek faulty/malicious application entire system ko damage kar sakti hai.

Isliye:

Normal apps restricted hoti hain, kernel highly privileged hota hai.

⚙️ 4. Operating System ke Main Duties
1. Process Management

OS:

Processes create karta hai
CPU time allocate karta hai
Processes schedule karta hai
Priority manage karta hai
Processes terminate karta hai
Example:

Browser + Music + Chat app simultaneously chalana.

Remember:

Process Management = Running programs ko manage karna

2. Memory Management

OS:

RAM allocate karta hai
Processes ko memory deta hai
Processes ki memory isolate karta hai
Unused memory reclaim karta hai
RAM low hone par virtual memory use kar sakta hai
Remember:

Memory Management = RAM ko efficiently manage karna

3. File Management

OS files/directories ko manage karta hai:

Files
Folders
Names
Paths
Permissions
Metadata
Timestamps

Example:

Folder banana ya file ko read-only karna.

4. User Management

OS:

User accounts manage karta hai
Authentication karta hai
Permissions assign karta hai

Example:

Login password ke through verify karna ki user kaun hai aur usko kya access hai.

5. Device Management

OS hardware devices ko manage karta hai.

Examples:

Mouse
Printer
Keyboard
External hard drive
Audio devices

Drivers aur hardware abstraction ke through applications ko devices use karne ka standard way milta hai.

🔐 5. OS Security Responsibilities

OS khud bhi security foundation provide karta hai.

Authentication

Tum kaun ho?

Example: Password, biometrics.

Permissions

Tum kya kar sakte ho?

Example: Read/write/execute access.

Isolation

Ek process ko doosre process se protected rakhna.

System Protection

Important system files/settings ko unauthorized changes se protect karna.

🖱️ 6. GUI vs CLI

OS se interact karne ke 2 major ways:

GUI — Graphical User Interface

Visual interface:

Windows
Icons
Folders
Menus
Buttons

Mostly mouse/touch se interact karte ho.

Example:

Folder par double-click → files dekhna.

GUI = Click-based interaction

CLI — Command-Line Interface

Text commands type karke OS ko instructions dete ho.

Example:

ls

Linux mein directory contents dekhne ke liye.

Advantages:
Precise
Fast
Powerful
Automation ke liye useful
Disadvantage:

Commands/syntax yaad hona chahiye.

CLI = Command-based interaction

🧠 GUI vs CLI Trick

GUI = Click 🖱️
CLI = Type ⌨️

Same kaam dono se kiya ja sakta hai, bas interaction ka method different hai.

